<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch11-section-ageostrophic-wind-derivation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Deriving the ageostrophic wind</title>

  <p>
    Recall from <xref ref="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis" text="custom">Chapter 8</xref>
    that the horizontal acceleration of air parcels making up the wind<mdash />given by
    <m>\dfrac{Du}{Dt}</m> in the zonal direction and
    <m>\dfrac{Dv}{Dt}</m> in the meridional direction<mdash />has the next-largest magnitude
    for mid-latitude synoptic scale analysis of the horizontal equations of motion: <m>\mathcal{O}(10^{-4} \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-2})</m>.
    Although this horizontal Lagrangian acceleration is about 10 times smaller than the horizontal pressure gradient and Coriolis accelerations
    for mid-latitude synoptic scale motions,
    retaining it allows for prediction and explanation of large-scale vertical motions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consider the horizontal equations of motion in height coordinates with these three largest accelerations retained:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-zonal" number="yes">
      \frac{Du}{Dt} = -\frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x} + fv
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-meridional" number="yes">
      \frac{Dv}{Dt} = -\frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y} - fu
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where the Coriolis parameter <m>f = 2 \Omega \sin{\phi}</m> has been substituted for simplicity.
    These equations can be combined into vector form as
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-vector" number="yes">
      \frac{D \vec{v}}{Dt} = -\frac{1}{\rho} \nabla_z p - f \, \hat{k} \times \vec{v}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where the "z" subscript emphasizes height coordinates are used and pressure is measured at constant height.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike the equations of geostrophic balance,
    which are <em>diagnostic</em>,
    <xref ref="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-zonal" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-vector" text="type-global" />
    are <em>prognostic</em> because they feature time derivatives through inclusion of horizontal Lagrangian acceleration.
    While they are more accurate to real atmospheric motions than the equations of geostrophic balance
    because the horizontal Lagrangian acceleration is retained,
    they still are approximate since smaller accelerations such as the curvature terms and viscous friction acceleration are neglected.
  </p>

  <p>
    Recall from <xref ref="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-geostrophic-balance" text="custom">Chapter 8</xref>
    that the zonal and meridional components of the geostrophic wind are given in height coordinates by
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-x-reproduced2" number="yes">
      u_g = -\frac{1}{\rho f} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    and
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-y-reproduced2" number="yes">
      v_g = \frac{1}{\rho f} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    respectively, and its vector equation is given by
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-vector-reproduced2" number="yes">
      \vec{v}_g = \frac{1}{\rho f} \, \hat{k} \times \nabla_z p
    </md>
  </p>

</section>